(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-chronic-vision-loss-vignettes"]={"title":"Chronic Vision Loss Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 3-year-old has a white pupillary reflex in one eye and an eye that has begun to turn. Which diagnosis leads?","choices":["Amblyopia","Toxocariasis","Retinoblastoma","Congenital cataract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leukocoria with strabismus is the classic presentation. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has a raised pigmented iris lesion with a feeder vessel that has grown. What is the next step?","choices":["Start topical steroid","Refer to ophthalmology, likely ocular oncology","Reassure and review in a year","Biopsy it in clinic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Growth and vascularity raise real concern. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with lupus on long-term prednisone reports glare and difficulty reading that eases when her pupils are dilated. Which cataract is this?","choices":["Nuclear","Cortical cataract","Zonular cataract","Posterior subcapsular"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Steroid-associated, faster, and classically under fifty. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with obesity has six weeks of headache, pulsatile tinnitus and papilledema. Brain imaging with venography is normal. What is the next step?","choices":["Start acetazolamide and review in a month","Repeat the scan with contrast","Reassure and treat as migraine","Lumbar puncture to measure opening pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Imaging first, then the pressure measurement that confirms it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman is found to have cupped discs on a routine examination but sees well. What happens next?","choices":["Refer to ophthalmology for assessment","Reassure, since her vision is normal","Repeat the examination in five years","Start drops without assessment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Good central vision does not exclude damage. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman has vertical diplopia that is worse looking down the stairs. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["Cranial nerve three","Cranial nerve two","Cranial nerve five","Cranial nerve four"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Trochlear palsy is worse on downgaze. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A child completed retinoblastoma treatment five years ago with no recurrence. What can the family be told?","choices":["Nothing can be said either way","He is considered cured","He remains in remission indefinitely","He needs lifelong chemotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is unusual among cancers. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man asks why melanoma of the ciliary body does worse than melanoma of the iris. Why?","choices":["It always involves the optic nerve","It is always bilateral","It is hidden, so it is found later","It is a more aggressive cell type"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An iris lesion is visible and caught earlier. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has 20/60 in the right eye and 20/25 in the left, with no ocular disease. What is the treatment?","choices":["Patch the right eye","Glasses alone and review in a year","No action until school age","Patch or atropinise the left eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The better eye is occluded to train the weaker one. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has reduced acuity and says colours look washed out. Which cause does that point to?","choices":["Optic neuropathy","Cataract","Open-angle glaucoma","Refractive error"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the one that takes colour vision. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman needs her intraocular pressure lowered. Which class works by reducing aqueous production?","choices":["Atropine drops","Beta blockers such as timolol","Prostaglandin analogues","Anti-VEGF injections"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as do carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has blur at all distances that improves fully with a toric lens. Which error does he have?","choices":["Amblyopia","Astigmatism","Myopia","Presbyopia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Uneven curvature gives multiple focal points. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman reports straight lines looking bent and a blur at the centre of her reading. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Nuclear cataract","Retinal detachment","Presbyopia with near blur","Age-related macular degeneration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central distortion is the hallmark. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A newborn has an absent red reflex in one eye. What happens next?","choices":["Urgent ophthalmology referral","Reassure, as reflexes vary","Refer to optometry","Repeat the examination in a month"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both retinoblastoma and congenital cataract present this way. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman asks what recovery after cataract surgery is like. What do you tell her?","choices":["The cataract commonly regrows","Prompt and near-complete in almost all isolated cases","It rarely improves vision","She will still need the same glasses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with complications infrequent overall. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy starts patching for amblyopia. What do you tell his parents about outcome?","choices":["Treatment rarely works at this age","Depth perception will be unaffected","Good, though not always fully normal vision","Vision will be entirely normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and starting before five is better still. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with known dry macular degeneration reports his central blur worsened sharply over a week. What is the concern?","choices":["Conversion to the wet form","A new cataract","Glaucoma developing","Refractive change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. New vessels bleeding cause a rapid change. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has flat, multifocal, bilateral surface pigment on both irides. What is this?","choices":["Melanocytoma","Corectopia","Iris freckles","Lisch nodules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surface pigment with no stromal involvement. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman's central vision has worsened suddenly and wet disease is suspected. Which test confirms it?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography or optical coherence tomography","Tonometry","Perimetry","Slit lamp examination alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both demonstrate the neovascular process. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A child has a retinal mass typical of retinoblastoma. Why is the diagnosis made without tissue?","choices":["Pathology cannot distinguish it","Imaging is definitive for all eye tumours","Parents usually decline biopsy","Biopsy risks seeding the tumour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Examination and imaging suffice. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has an enlarging pigmented conjunctival lesion. What is the next step?","choices":["Prescribe lubricating drops","Treat as allergic conjunctivitis","Refer to ophthalmology for assessment","Reassure, as conjunctival pigment is common"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Growth in a pigmented lesion demands specialist review. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man treated for choroidal melanoma asks what follow-up he needs. What do you tell him?","choices":["Surveillance for liver metastasis","Yearly chest imaging only","Examination of the other eye only","No follow-up once treated locally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The liver is where it goes. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has an eyelid haemangioma covering the visual axis. What is the priority?","choices":["Observe until school age","Treat only if it bleeds","Reassure that it will involute","Prompt referral to prevent amblyopia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The obstruction has to be cleared while vision can still develop. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has sudden ptosis, a dilated pupil and an eye deviated down and out. What must be excluded urgently?","choices":["Amblyopia","An aneurysm compressing the third nerve","Myasthenia gravis","Thyroid eye disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pupil involvement makes this an emergency. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl cannot read the whiteboard but reads a book comfortably. What is the next step?","choices":["Refer to optometry for refraction","Refer urgently to ophthalmology","Arrange brain imaging","Patch the better eye"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An isolated refractive error is their work. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with idiopathic intracranial hypertension asks what will keep it from coming back. What do you tell her?","choices":["Sustained weight reduction","Lifelong acetazolamide","Yearly lumbar punctures","Blood pressure control alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medication covers her while that happens. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has papilledema and headache. Why is venography included with her scan?","choices":["To look for multiple sclerosis","To assess the carotid arteries","To exclude venous sinus thrombosis","To measure the opening pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A clot raises pressure and mimics the idiopathic form. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with a stable iris nevus asks whether it will become cancer. What do you tell her?","choices":["Risk depends on her eye colour","The risk of transformation is low","It will certainly become melanoma","There is no risk at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, but surveillance continues because it is not zero. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A toddler cries only when his right eye is covered. What does the examiner conclude?","choices":["He has a squint on the right","The test is uninterpretable","The left eye sees poorly","The right eye sees poorly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He objects when his good eye is taken away. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with a nuclear cataract finds he can suddenly read without glasses. Why?","choices":["His macula has improved","The lens has shifted him myopic","His presbyopia has resolved","He has developed hyperopia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nuclear change blurs distance more than near. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
